 because you're currently touching those files\n- Semantic + error are content signals — memory must be topically relevant\n- Recency/importance only \"rescue\" memories if they have meaningful content\n- This prevents spammy injection of loosely-related recent memories\n\n**Example from README:**\n```\nPrompt: \"server returning unauthorized when trying to purchase items\"\n\nMemory A: \"Cart 401 debug — CF_KV_NAMESPACE_ID changed after admin recreation\"\n  file_overlap: 23%, semantic: 78%, error_match: 100%, recency: 92%, importance: 100%\n  → score: 68% → INJECTED ✓\n\nMemory B: \"Fix video styling on homepage\"\n  file_overlap: 0%, semantic: 3%, error_match: 0%, recency: 98%, importance: 10%\n  → score: 12% → FILTERED OUT ✓\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. **OUTCOME TRACKING & LIFECYCLE**\n\n**No formal outcome tracking exists yet**, but the architecture supports it:\n\n**Memory Lifecycle** (inferred from code):\n1. **Create:** Distilled on commit via post-commit hook\n2. **Activate:** Injected when relevance scoring triggers (threshold-based)\n3. **Suppress:** Can be manually deleted from `.agent-memory/*.json`\n4. **Archive:** Could be moved to cold storage based on age\n5. **Prune:** `agent-memory stats` shows total memories; manual cleanup possible\n\n**What's NOT tracked:**\n- Did the agent actually read the memory?\n- Did the memory help solve the problem?\n- Was the memory accurate or did it mislead?\n\nThis is a known gap — the system doesn't currently have feedback loops to:\n- Mark memories as \"helpful\" or \"misleading\"\n- Rank memories by historical helpfulness\n- Down-rank memories that led to wasted effort\n\n---\n\n### 8. **SCOPES SUPPORTED**\n\n**Repository-wide scope** — Agent Memory operates at the repo level:\n- Stores memories in `.agent-memory/` (one repo = one memory store)\n- No team scope, no personal scope, no cross-repo search\n- However, memories preserve branch context in `memory.branch` field\n\n**Per-session tagging** via `generateTags()`:\n- File names and directories\n- Error patterns (auth, deployment, database, etc.)\n- Can search/filter by tags in CLI\n\n**CLI commands** that show scope:\n- `agent-memory list` — all memories in repo\n- `agent-memory recall <prompt>` — search across all memories\n- `agent-memory stats` — aggregate stats (branches touched, files, decisions)\n\n---\n\n### 9. **MEMORY LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT**\n\n**Current implementation:**\n- **Create:** Automatic on `git commit` (post-commit hook)\n- **Activate:** Automatic on new Claude Code session (UserPromptSubmit hook)\n- **View:** `agent-memory show <checkpoint-id>`\n- **List:** `agent-memory list` shows all with date, file count, decision count\n- **Delete:** Not implemented; would be manual file deletion\n- **Archive:** Not implemented; no time-based pruning\n\n**Observations from git log:**\n- Recent commits show performance optimizations (cut inject time from 22s → 13s by replacing LLM query refinement with instant regex)\n- Quality gates added to prevent irrelevant memory injection\n- Session-ID tracking to avoid duplicate injections\n\n---\n\n### 10. **CLI COMMANDS & API**\n\n**File:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/cli.ts`\n\n| Command | Purpose | Output |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| `agent-memory install` | One-time setup — installs both git and Claude Code hooks | Success messages |\n| `agent-memory uninstall` | Remove hooks | Confirmation |\n| `agent-memory status` | Check hook status, memory count, undistilled checkpoints | Table with installed hooks + counts |\n| `agent-memory distill <id>` | Distill single checkpoint | JSON memory record |\n| `agent-memory distill --all` | Distill all checkpoints in repo | Line-by-line progress + summary |\n| `agent-memory recall <prompt>` | Find relevant memories for prompt | Scored list with breakdown |\n| `agent-memory inject <prompt>` | Output formatted context block for agent | Developer summary + full memory block |\n| `agent-memory list` | List all stored memories | Table: date, id, files, decisions, intent |\n| `agent-memory show <id>` | View full memory record | Pretty-printed JSON |\n| `agent-memory stats` | Repository memory statistics | Aggregate counts: decisions, warnings, branches, files |\n\n**Programmatic API:**\n- `distill(repoPath, checkpointId, memoriesDir)` — produces MemoryRecord\n- `retrieve(store, context, topK, memoriesDir)` — returns ScoredMemory[]\n- `formatForInjection(memories)` — text block for agents\n- `scoreMemory(memory, context, embeddingSimilarity, errorCodes)` — returns ScoredMemory\n\n---\n\n### 11. **WHAT'S NOVEL OR UNIQUE**\n\n**Compared to naive memory systems:**\n\n1. **Multi-signal relevance scoring** (not just keyword match):\n   - File overlap + semantic + error signature + recency + importance\n   - Quality gates prevent spammy injection\n   - Content relevance must be ≥30% before other signals rescue memory\n\n2. **Dual-mode distillation**:\n   - LLM-powered (Claude Sonnet) for high-quality extraction when available\n   - Heuristic fallback (regex patterns) for offline/fast distillation\n   - Cause chains capture full debugging narrative (symptom → misdiagnosis → root cause)\n\n3. **Integration with Entire.io checkpoints**:\n   - Reads from Entire's git-native checkpoint storage\n   - Handles multiple sub-indices per session (allows resuming sessions mid-work)\n   - No custom checkpoint format — reuses Entire's JSONL directly\n\n4. **Embedding-based semantic matching**:\n   - Uses Hugging Face Inference API (384-dim all-MiniLM-L6-v2 vectors)\n   - Zero native dependencies\n   - Falls back to keyword matching if embeddings unavailable\n\n5. **Fast, zero-latency injection**:\n   - Recent optimization: replaced LLM query refinement with instant regex extraction\n   - Inject time cut from 22s → 13s\n   - Uses session tracking to avoid re-injecting same memories\n\n6. **Structured memory format**:\n   - 9 actionable fields (not just raw transcript)\n   - Clear distinction between decisions/failures/warnings\n   - File dependencies capture non-obvious coupling rules\n\n7. **Complementary to Entire's semantic layer**:\n   - Agent Memory = fast, structured, ~200 tokens per memory\n   - Entire = deep, raw, ~50KB per session\n   - Together: index for fast lookup + deep context for detailed investigation\n\n---\n\n### 12. **INTERESTING ALGORITHMS & PATTERNS**\n\n**A. Exponential Decay for Recency** (`scorer.ts` line 154):\n```typescript\nreturn Math.max(0, 100 * Math.exp(-0.05 * daysAgo));\n```\n- Memories lose value over time\n- Weights: 1 day ago = 95%, 7 days = 70%, 30 days = 22%\n- Recent memories preferred but still useful if relevant\n\n**B. Cosine Similarity for Embeddings** (`embeddings.ts` lines 70-87):\n```typescript\ndotProduct / (sqrt(normA) * sqrt(normB))\n```\n- Normalizes by magnitude — direction matters, not scale\n- Perfect for comparing high-dimensional vectors\n- Returns 0-1 range, scales directly to percentage\n\n**C. Configurable Boost Factor** (`scorer.ts` lines 35-39):\n```typescript\nconst boostFactor = contentRelevance >= 25 ? 1.0 : contentRelevance / 25;\nconst score = errorMatch > 0\n  ? fileOverlap * 0.3 + semantic * 0.25 + errorMatch * 0.2 \n    + (recency * boostFactor) * 0.15 + (importance * boostFactor) * 0.1\n```\n- If semantic/error < 25%, recency/importance get diminished influence\n- Prevents old high-value memories from drowning out new relevant ones\n- Prevents recent-but-irrelevant from being injected\n\n**D. Error Signature Exact Matching** (`scorer.ts` lines 130-144):\n```typescript\nconst sigText = signatures.join(\" \").toLowerCase();\nlet matches = 0;\nfor (const code of errorCodes) {\n  if (sigText.includes(code.toLowerCase())) matches++;\n}\n```\n- Highest-precision signal — if you're debugging \"401\", match against memories with \"401\"\n- No fuzzy matching — exact string search\n- Used when LLM extracts error codes from raw prompt\n\n**E. File Name Normalization** (`scorer.ts` lines 63-77):\n```typescript\nconst memoryNames = new Set(\n  memoryFiles.filter(isRelevantFile).map(f => f.split(\"/\").pop()!.toLowerCase())\n);\n```\n- Compares only filenames, ignoring paths\n- Filters out config files (.git, .claude, node_modules) that pollute scoring\n- Handles cross-directory renames gracefully\n\n**F. Heuristic Extraction with Limited Results** (`distiller/index.ts` lines 221-244):\n```typescript\nconst decisions: string[] = [];\nfor (const msg of messages) {\n  const sentences = msg.split(/[.!]\\s+/);\n  for (const sentence of sentences) {\n    for (const pattern of decisionPatterns) {\n      const match = sentence.match(pattern);\n      if (match && match[1] && match[1].length > 20 && match[1].length < 200) {\n        decisions.push(match[1].trim());\n        break; // One per sentence\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\nreturn [...new Set(decisions)].slice(0, 5); // Max 5, deduplicated\n```\n- Deduplicates across entire session\n- Length constraints (20-200 chars) filter noise\n- Limited to 5 per field prevents bloat\n\n**G. Query Refinement Patterns** (`retriever/query.ts` lines 42-82):\n- Extracts HTTP status codes: `/\\b[1-5]\\d{2}\\b/g`\n- Extracts error types: `TypeError`, `ReferenceError`, `CORS`, `ECONNREFUSED`\n- Extracts file hints: `[\\w\\-]+\\.\\w{1,4}` with extension whitelist\n- All regex-based for instant (~1ms) execution\n\n---\n\n### 13. **KEY DESIGN DECISIONS & TRADEOFFS**\n\nFrom git history and code:\n\n**Tradeoff: Speed vs. Quality**\n- Recent commit: Replaced LLM query refinement with regex extraction\n- Reason: 22 seconds → 13 seconds (hooks must be fast)\n- Result: Fast-enough accuracy for error/file extraction\n\n**Tradeoff: File-based storage vs. Database**\n- File system: Simple, no server dependency, git-friendly\n- Database: Would be faster at scale (1000+ memories)\n- Choice: File-based for simplicity, satisfactory performance\n\n**Tradeoff: 5 signals vs. Single score**\n- Multiple signals allow different relevance modes (error matching, file overlap)\n- Complex scoring but more nuanced results\n- Quality gates implemented to prevent gaming\n\n**Tradeoff: Optional LLM vs. Always required**\n- LLM distillation is optional (heuristics work offline)\n- Embeddings are optional (keyword matching fallback)\n- Philosophy: Be useful even without external services\n\n**Tradeoff: Per-memory quality gates vs. Top-K cutoff**\n- Not just \"take top 5\" — filter each individually\n- Prevents weak memories from injecting alongside strong ones\n- More complex but prevents bad results\n\n---\n\n### 14. **WHAT WOULD BE USEFUL FOR YOUR MEMORY LOOP**\n\nBased on this analysis, here are patterns worth adopting:\n\n1. **Multi-signal scoring** — Don't rely on single relevance metric. File overlap + semantic + error codes + recency + importance gives nuanced ranking.\n\n2. **Quality gates per item** — Not just threshold filtering; evaluate each memory individually for content relevance. Prevents weak results.\n\n3. **Dual-mode extraction** — Support both LLM and heuristic fallback. Makes system work offline and handles cold starts.\n\n4. **Structured memory format** — 9 fields per memory (intent, decisions, failures, warnings, etc.) is more actionable than storing raw transcripts.\n\n5. **Fast injection path** — Optimize hook execution to <1s. Current perf: 13s is still too slow; agent-memory is working on sub-second query refinement.\n\n6. **Error signature exact matching** — When users mention errors, exact string matching on stored error codes is high-precision. Worth weighting heavily.\n\n7. **Embedding vectors + keyword fallback** — Store embeddings for semantic matching but don't require them. Gives graceful degradation.\n\n8. **Session-level deduplication** — Track session IDs to avoid re-injecting same memories. Prevents redundancy in same conversation.\n\n9. **Embedding store caching** — Store vectors in JSON alongside memories. Regenerating on every retrieval is slow.\n\n10. **Cause chains over root causes alone** — Capture full debugging path (symptom → wrong diagnosis → actual cause) not just final answer. Helps agents avoid same dead ends.\n\n---\n\n### **FILE PATHS FOR REFERENCE**\n\nKey files:\n\n- **Main entry:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/cli.ts`\n- **Data structures:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/types/index.ts`\n- **Storage:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/store/index.ts`\n- **Distillation:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/distiller/index.ts`\n- **LLM integration:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/distiller/llm.ts`\n- **Heuristic extraction:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/distiller/extractor.ts`\n- **Retrieval core:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/retriever/index.ts`\n- **Scoring algorithm:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/retriever/scorer.ts`\n- **Query refinement:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/retriever/query.ts`\n- **Embeddings:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/retriever/embeddings.ts`\n- **Injection formatting:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/injector/index.ts`\n- **Git hooks:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/hooks/index.ts`\n- **Claude Code hooks:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/src/hooks/claude-code.ts`\n- **Tests:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/tests/` (66 tests, well-structured)\n- **README:** `/Users/alisha/Projects/agent-memory/README.md` (excellent documentation)\n\n**Code statistics:** 2,105 lines of TypeScript across all modules, ~66 passing tests, production-ready."}],"totalDurationMs":2505552,"totalTokens":75730,"totalToolUseCount":26,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1817,"cache_read_input_tokens":67914,"output_tokens":5995,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1817},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cc027b3a-e77c